This is an entire program. There is no `main` — the attribute generates it.

```rust
#![feature(tail_core)]

use periodic::periodic;

/// A sensor reading. `repr(C)` and `Copy` because it crosses shared memory.
#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
#[repr(C)]
struct Range {
    stamp: u64,
    range_mm: u32,
    quality: u32,
}

// SAFETY: `repr(C)`, all-integer, every bit pattern valid.
unsafe impl std::tail_core::sync::shared_mutex::ShmSafe for Range {}

#[periodic(every = "20ms", publish = "sensor/range", lane = safe, on_overrun = Skip)]
fn lidar() -> Range {
    Range { stamp: 0x1234_5678, range_mm: 4200, quality: 97 }
}
```

The framework opens the topic — retrying, so startup order is not the application's
problem — releases the body at `start + n * period`, publishes the return value, and
counts every release the body ran past.

Add the crate as a direct dependency:

```toml
[dependencies]
periodic = { path = "<relative path to>/framework/periodic/lib" }
```

`periodic` is a standalone direct dependency, not re-exported through `tail_core`, so
adding it forces no toolchain or `std` rebuild.

A publishing node's return type decides whether it may skip a cycle. No attribute key
selects between them; the compiler picks from the return type.

```rust
// Always publishes.
#[periodic(every = "20ms", publish = "sensor/range", lane = safe, on_overrun = Skip)]
fn lidar() -> Range {
    read_sensor()
}

// Publishes only when it has something to say.
#[periodic(every = "20ms", publish = "sensor/range", lane = safe, on_overrun = Skip)]
fn lidar() -> Option<Range> {
    read_sensor_if_ready()
}
```

Prefer `T` when the node always produces a sample. `Option<T>` costs a copy that `T` does
not: a node that may decline has to decide before entering the fill path, which forces the
value onto the stack first.

Returning `None` is **not** an overrun and is not counted as one. "I had nothing to say"
and "I did not finish in time" are different facts, reported through different fields.

A node that must keep something between cycles — a device handle, a filter, an
accumulator — names how to build it with `init` and takes it as its **first** parameter,
by `&mut`.

```rust
use periodic::periodic;

#[derive(Default)]
struct Cycles(u64);

#[periodic(every = "20ms", publish = "diag/counted", lane = safe, on_overrun = Skip,
           init = Cycles::default())]
fn counter(cycles: &mut Cycles) -> Count {
    cycles.0 += 1;
    Count { cycles: cycles.0 }
}
```

The state is built once, before the first release, and the same one is handed back on
every cycle. It is dropped when the node stops.

An actuator holding a device handle is the ordinary shape, not an exception. Without
`init` such a node would reach for a `static`, which is the boilerplate the framework
exists to remove.

With both `init` and `subscribe`, state comes first:

```rust
#[periodic(every = "5ms", subscribe = "input/pad", lane = kernel, stale_after = "300ms",
           on_overrun = Skip, init = Motor::attach())]
fn drive(motor: &mut Motor, pad: Cycle<Command>) {
    motor.set(pad.get().map_or(Command::Stop, |command| *command));
}
```

